<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Universal Links. BOTH hosts are claimed, and that is not belt and braces; it is required.
       The app builds every share link from RC_SHARE_ORIGIN, which is pinned to
       https://rounds-codex.netlify.app . Claiming only roundscodex.com would mean a link shared
       FROM the app opens in Safari rather than in the app: the commonest case, silently broken,
       with iOS reporting nothing.
       CORRECTED 2026-09-14: this comment used to say both hosts serve the same Netlify site and
       that one apple-app-site-association therefore answers for both. That is FALSE, measured
       through the Netlify API: roundscodex.com is served by the project `roundscodexwebsite`
       (bf814a35-8afd-4f7c-8bde-4b23566409ea) and the app by `rounds-codex`
       (15778795-d2c2-4196-a2d5-fdaa5657a573). Two sites, two document roots.
       So the netlify.app line is the one carrying the feature. Verified in production on that host:
       200 application/json, zero redirects, appID 744JSM2Z3H.com.roundscodex.app, routes matching
       the app's own RC_ROOT and RC_OPEN_ROUTES regexes (scripts/verify_universal_links.js).
       The two roundscodex.com lines are inert until that domain serves this app's AASA. Harmless,
       and worth keeping for the day the brand domain becomes canonical.
       When roundscodex.com becomes canonical and RC_SHARE_ORIGIN moves, the netlify.app line can
       go, but not before; old shared links will keep arriving at it for a long time.
       NOTE: no double hyphen anywhere in this comment. XML forbids it inside a comment, and a
       plist that will not parse is a build failure rather than a warning. -->
  <key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
  <array>
    <string>applinks:roundscodex.com</string>
    <string>applinks:www.roundscodex.com</string>
    <string>applinks:rounds-codex.netlify.app</string>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
